# Break even when the answer changes twice.
#
# Everything paid over 5 years is not always
# monotonic. For "A", a longer amortization means
# a smaller payment, but a short one clears the
# loan inside the 5 years and stops the payments.
# So against "B", a 3 year loan, the answer to
# "does A pay less over 5 years?" changes twice,
# at 3 years and at 5 years 4 months, and Renew
# lists both instead of picking one.
#
# The second pair crosses twice only 29 cents
# apart: paid over 5 years rises with the yearly
# lump until the loan is paid off inside the 5
# years, then falls. The money search covers
# every cent if the bound in docs/LANGUAGE.md
# holds, so both changes are found.
# The second change is a one cent difference:
# at $15,997.97 "Base" pays $114,884.39 against
# $114,884.40. It is real once every amount is
# rounded to the cent, but it is a rounding
# edge more than a money decision.
#
# Both lines use the short form of break even:
# break even SETTING "A" vs "B". The long form,
# break even SETTING for "A" against "B", is the
# same statement.

mortgage "A"
  balance $400,000
  rate 5%
  amortization 25 years
  term 1 year

mortgage "B"
  balance $400,000
  rate 5%
  amortization 3 years
  term 3 years

show break even amortization "A" vs "B" by paid over 5 years

mortgage "Base"
  balance $100,000
  rate 5%
  amortization 25 years
  term 5 years
  lump $0 every year

mortgage "Ref"
  balance $329,215.72
  rate 5%
  amortization 25 years
  term 5 years

show paid of "Ref"
show break even lump "Base" vs "Ref" by paid over 5 years
